<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the generated Info.plist by Tauri's macOS bundler.

  `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` is not documentation and not a nicety. On
  macOS a process that touches the microphone without this key is **killed by
  the operating system** the instant it does so — not an error, not a denied
  permission, a termination. The app would have died on the first press of
  Record with nothing in the UI to explain it.

  There is deliberately no key here for Screen Recording, which is what
  ScreenCaptureKit needs to capture the other side of a call. macOS has no
  usage-description key for that permission: it is granted in System Settings
  and there is nothing an app can put in its plist to ask for it. The
  plain-English explanation therefore has to come from our own code, and it
  does — see `platform/src/macos/speaker.rs`.

  Both strings are written for someone who is not an engineer, because these
  are read inside a system dialog by a person deciding whether to trust the
  app. They also state the thing that matters most about this product: nothing
  leaves the machine.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Notetaker needs the microphone to record your meetings and classes. Recordings are transcribed on this computer and are never sent anywhere.</string>

	<!--
	  Recording the sound of a call means capturing what the speakers are
	  playing, which macOS treats as screen recording even though Notetaker
	  never looks at the picture.
	-->
	<key>NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Notetaker needs permission to record this computer's sound so it can hear everyone else on a call. It only captures audio, never the picture on your screen.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
